From 2623f69bacdf02e82e9935bf9851cc7b4c89fc2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Implement SUBQUERY in expressions and in the predicate parser

NSSubqueryExpressionType is named in the enumeration and
GSSubqueryExpression is declared with an empty implementation, but there
is nothing behind either: no
+expressionForSubquery:usingIteratorVariable:predicate:, no evaluation,
and nothing in the parser. SUBQUERY(...) in a format string is read as a
call to a function named SUBQUERY and raises while parsing.

The raise comes from somewhere unexpected - "[GSVariableExpression
-keyPath] should be overridden by subclass" - and that is a second gap
behind the first: the parser asks an expression for its key path to
decide what it is looking at, and -keyPath raises for the kinds that
have none. $x.y could not be parsed either, subquery or no subquery.

Add the factory method, the expression class (evaluation, description,
coding, equality and substitution) and the parser clause, and make the
parser ask an expression for its kind before it asks for a key path.

The iterator variable is bound by substituting it into the subpredicate
for each member in turn, since -evaluateWithObject: passes no context
for a variable to be looked up in. The member is also the object the
subpredicate is evaluated against, so a key path inside it may be
written either way round. The iterator variable belongs to the
subquery, so a substitution of variables from outside leaves it alone.

The NSPredicate test suite (214 tests) passes with the change.

The test added here cannot run on an unpatched library: parsing
"$f.age > 40" raises before the subquery is reached.
---
 Headers/Foundation/NSExpression.h |   6 +
 Source/NSPredicate.m              | 253 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 Tests/base/NSPredicate/subquery.m |  88 +++++++++++
 3 files changed, 344 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 Tests/base/NSPredicate/subquery.m

